20:09:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: They can remove zeros. It’s called **redenomination**. For example, Iraq could declare: - **1,000 old dinars = 1 new dinar** - A 14,000 IQD item becomes 14 new IQD. - A salary of 1,400,000 IQD becomes 1,400 new IQD. - The exchange rate changes from roughly 1,400 old IQD per US$1 to roughly 1.4 new IQD per US$1. Nothing real has changed: prices, wages, savings, debts, and exchange rates are all divided by the same amount. A person who could buy $100 worth of goods before can still buy $100 worth afterward. Also, “1,400 dinars per dollar” does not mean the dinar is “high-valued.” It means one individual dinar unit is worth relatively little compared with one US dollar. The number of currency units per dollar is largely a matter of how the country chose to define its unit. Countries may avoid redenomination because it has costs and risks: - New banknotes, coins, ATM software, accounting systems, contracts, price labels, and tax records must be updated. - It can confuse people, especially older or less financially literate populations. - Businesses may use the transition to round prices upward. - It can create opportunities for scams or counterfeit currency. - If inflation is still high or policy is not credible, zeros may simply return later. Many countries have done it successfully, including Turkey in 2005, when it removed six zeros from the lira. But it works best after inflation and economic instability are under control. Removing zeros is mostly an administrative convenience—not a way to make a country or its currency genuinely richer.
