20:10:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There usually is not a technical barrier. The main reason is that removing zeros often solves a **cosmetic inconvenience**, while creating a large, expensive transition project. A country asks: “Is changing every banknote, price tag, contract, accounting system, tax form, ATM, payroll system, and government database worth it just to make the numbers shorter?” Often the answer is: **not yet**. A few reasons countries leave it alone for decades: 1. **The exchange-rate number itself is not economically important.** “1,400 dinars per US dollar” sounds dramatic, but it is just the chosen size of the currency unit. Japan has often had roughly 100–160 yen per dollar; South Korea has often had over 1,000 won per dollar. Those currencies can function perfectly normally. 2. **People and businesses adapt.** They may quote prices in thousands or millions, use shorthand, rely on electronic payments, or simply use higher-denomination notes. If everyone understands that “20” informally means “20,000,” the inconvenience may be manageable. 3. **Redenomination is administratively risky.** During a conversion, both old and new money may circulate at once. Shops must display dual prices, banks must convert balances correctly, software must work, and contracts and debts must be interpreted consistently. Mistakes can hurt ordinary people. 4. **It can be politically unpopular or low priority.** Governments dealing with unemployment, conflict, debt, weak banking systems, corruption, or inflation may not devote major political attention and public money to redesigning the unit of account. 5. **It only makes sense once inflation is under control.** If a country removes six zeros but then continues to have high inflation, new zeros soon reappear. That makes the reform embarrassing and undermines trust. So governments often wait until they can credibly maintain stable prices. 6. **Sometimes the currency is not the main unit people use anyway.** In heavily dollarized economies, large purchases, imports, savings, and property may already be priced in US dollars or euros. Redenominating the local currency does not fix the deeper lack of trust in it. For Iraq specifically, a rate around 1,300–1,500 dinars per US dollar is not, by itself, an emergency-level figure. It mostly reflects the historical size of the dinar unit and past inflation/devaluations. Iraq could declare, for example, 1,000 old dinars = 1 new dinar, but that would not strengthen the economy, increase purchasing power, or make imports cheaper. So yes, something is “stopping” them—but it is usually not impossibility. It is the calculation that the benefits are modest unless the large numbers are seriously disrupting everyday commerce, and that stable economic policy matters far more than the number of zeros printed on the notes.
