20:10:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes—this is the important distinction: **Removing zeros is mechanically simple; making the currency stable enough for the removed zeros to stay gone is not.** Zimbabwe is a good example. It did repeatedly remove zeros: - In 2006, it removed 3 zeros. - In 2008, it removed 10 zeros. - In 2009, it removed 12 zeros. But inflation was exploding because the government was effectively financing spending by creating money while production, confidence, foreign-currency reserves, and the tax base had collapsed. So prices kept rising at extraordinary rates. Removing zeros only changed the labels; it did not stop the underlying process that was creating more zeros. If bread rises from 1,000,000 to 1,000,000,000 currency units because of inflation, changing the unit so it reads “1” today does nothing if it will read “1,000” a few months later. Eventually Zimbabwe largely abandoned its old currency and used foreign currencies—especially the US dollar and South African rand—because people no longer trusted the local money as a store of value or a unit of account. A local currency was later reintroduced, but maintaining confidence has remained difficult. Vietnam is different. The Vietnamese dong has a large number of units per US dollar—roughly 25,000 VND per US$1—but it has not had Zimbabwe-style runaway inflation for decades. The large number mostly reflects its historical currency unit and past inflation, not an ongoing monetary collapse. Vietnam could redenominate, for example by making 10,000 old dong equal 1 new dong, but it has generally judged the disruption not worth the benefit. So there are two separate situations: | Situation | Is removing zeros easy? | Does it solve the problem? | |---|---:|---:| | Stable country with an awkwardly small unit, such as Vietnam | Yes | It can improve convenience, but changes little economically | | Hyperinflationary country, such as Zimbabwe at its worst | Yes in theory, difficult in practice | No—unless the government also stops the inflationary causes | What makes it hard in a Zimbabwe-type case is not printing replacement notes. It is achieving a credible broader stabilization program, such as: - ending routine money creation to fund government deficits; - restoring tax revenue and controlling spending; - rebuilding productive capacity and access to imports; - obtaining foreign-exchange reserves; - making the central bank credible and operationally independent; - stabilizing banks and the financial system; - convincing the public that the new currency will retain value. That last part is especially hard. Once people have been burned by repeated currency collapses, they save and price things in dollars, gold, foreign currency, or goods. Even if the government issues a “new” currency with fewer zeros, people may immediately exchange it for dollars because they expect another devaluation. That expectation itself puts pressure on the currency. So your instinct is right: it is not simply a free, consequence-free button governments can press. **The accounting conversion is simple; the political, fiscal, institutional, and trust problem behind persistent zero growth is extremely hard.**
