20:11:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There is not a hidden rule preventing it. The missing point is that a large exchange-rate number is usually **far less burdensome than it looks from abroad**. A country such as Vietnam does not experience “25,000 dong per US dollar” every time someone buys lunch. People use familiar denominations, shorthand, and local price conventions. A 500,000-dong note is simply understood as a high-value note, much as a ¥10,000 note is in Japan. The number of dong per dollar is not a daily economic problem demanding repair. So the real barrier is not merely vague “political will.” It is a lopsided cost-benefit calculation: - **Benefit:** shorter numbers, easier spreadsheets, perhaps slightly simpler accounting. - **Cost/risk:** replace cash, modify systems and laws, convert every account and contract, run dual-price periods, educate the public, prevent fraud and rounding abuses, and absorb confusion and political suspicion. If nothing goes wrong by leaving the system alone, the government gets little credit for changing it. If even a small part of a redenomination goes wrong—people are overcharged, pension payments are misconverted, scammers exploit elderly people, or rumors spread that savings are being confiscated—it becomes a major political problem. That creates a strong “don’t fix what isn’t broken” bias. There is also an important distinction: - A currency having **many units per dollar** is not inherently a sign of weakness. - A currency that is **continually losing purchasing power** is the real problem. Vietnam’s dong has depreciated over long periods and has had inflation at times, but it is not in the Zimbabwe-style situation where prices are rapidly accumulating zeros every year. Its current large unit is mostly an inherited convention. Iraq’s dinar similarly reflects history, including past inflation and devaluations; its roughly 1,300–1,500 dinars per dollar does not by itself make normal commerce unworkable. And it is not true that countries never do this after achieving stability. Examples include: - **Turkey**, 2005: removed six zeros after bringing inflation down. - **Romania**, 2005: removed four zeros. - **Ghana**, 2007: removed four zeros. - **Mozambique**, 2006: removed three zeros. - **Bulgaria**, 1999: removed three zeros. So why not Vietnam or Iraq? Mainly because their governments have apparently judged that the gain is too small relative to the disruption—especially given other priorities. Iraq has additionally had political instability, substantial cash usage, limited banking access for many people, dollar use in parts of the economy, and public-trust issues. A neutral conversion could easily be perceived as a trick, devaluation, or seizure. Vietnam has reportedly considered redenomination proposals in the past, but not carried them through. That itself is telling: it is feasible, but policymakers did not see enough advantage to justify the transition. The closest analogy is changing from measuring everyday distance in millimeters to centimeters. It may make some figures look nicer, but it does not make anyone richer, lower prices, strengthen exports, or improve monetary policy. Unless the old measurement system is causing serious problems, people tend to keep using the familiar one.
