20:11:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It suggests something more specific than “just political will,” but not a permanent economic law. The key missing idea is: > **Countries redenominate when the existing unit is seriously inconvenient in domestic life—not merely because the exchange rate looks large against the US dollar.** Turkey and Romania crossed that threshold. Vietnam and Iraq generally have not. ### Turkey’s old lira was much more cumbersome Before Turkey’s 2005 redenomination, roughly **1.3 million old lira equaled US$1**. Ordinary prices, salaries, invoices, accounting entries, and government budgets routinely involved millions, billions, and trillions of lira. Removing six zeros made a major practical difference. Vietnam’s rate of roughly **25,000 dong per US$1** is much less extreme in that sense. South Korea has roughly 1,000+ won per dollar; Indonesia has roughly 15,000+ rupiah per dollar. Those are large-looking foreign-exchange numbers, but not necessarily unworkable domestic units. Iraq at about **1,400 dinars per dollar** is even less extreme. It is broadly in the “Korean won / Japanese yen” range of unit sizing, not a Turkey-pre-2005 or Zimbabwe situation. ### The denomination problem matters A redenomination must leave prices and cash denominations usable. Suppose Iraq removed three zeros: - 1,000 old dinars = 1 new dinar - US$1 ≈ 1.4 new dinars Then a 25,000-dinar note—the largest commonly used Iraqi note—would become a 25-new-dinar note, worth roughly US$17–18. That can work, but everyday purchases could start needing decimals or small coins: - A US$0.70 item might cost about 1 new dinar. - A US$0.07 item might cost 0.1 new dinar. Coins are often unpopular and expensive to circulate in cash-heavy economies. Iraq could remove only two zeros instead, but then it gets less of the “zero removal” benefit. There is no obviously perfect new scale. Vietnam could remove three zeros: - 1,000 old dong = 1 new dong - US$1 ≈ 25 new dong. That is technically workable, but it would turn common prices such as 20,000–50,000 dong into 20–50 new dong. Nice-looking numbers, yes—but not a transformative gain worth a nationwide conversion unless current numbers are causing real problems. ### “Stable” does not mean the same thing in every country Vietnam has had much more monetary stability than Zimbabwe, but its currency is still managed, has depreciated gradually over long periods, and has had periods of meaningful inflation. The authorities may prefer to retain a familiar unit until they are confident they will not need another adjustment later. Iraq is even less comparable to Turkey or Romania at the time they redenominated. Its official exchange rate may look fairly stable, but its monetary environment has included: - strong dependence on oil revenue and foreign-currency inflows; - widespread use of cash and dollars; - informal/parallel foreign-exchange markets; - uneven banking access; - political and security disruption; - public sensitivity to anything involving currency conversion. A stable official exchange rate does not automatically mean a government has the institutional confidence to tell everyone, “Bring in your cash, convert all contracts, and trust us that nothing is being taken from you.” ### Turkey and Romania also had unusually strong reasons to act Their redenominations were part of broader stabilization and modernization programs. - **Turkey** had recently brought down very high chronic inflation and wanted a visible break with the old inflationary era. - **Romania** redenominated during a period of economic reform and preparation for closer European integration. In those cases, redenomination was not only about shorter numbers. It was a public signal: *the old inflationary regime is over; the state can now run a credible currency.* Vietnam and Iraq may not see the same political payoff. For them, it could instead draw attention to past depreciation, trigger rumors of devaluation or confiscation, and create a major administrative burden without solving a pressing economic problem. ### So is there a “permanent blocker”? No. But there is a durable **status-quo equilibrium**: 1. People already understand the existing unit. 2. The banking, tax, retail, and legal systems already use it. 3. Large exchange-rate numbers do not necessarily impair normal domestic transactions. 4. Conversion creates real risks of errors, fraud, rounding, and public suspicion. 5. The government gains little if it succeeds, but takes blame if anything goes wrong. That can keep a currency unchanged for decades. So the more precise answer is: Vietnam and Iraq *can* remove zeros, and neither is physically or economically prevented from doing so. But unlike Turkey’s pre-2005 lira, their present unit sizes are not inconvenient enough to make the benefits clearly outweigh the transition costs and trust risks.
