20:12:24 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=3205 miss=1083
20:12:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No. There is no economic law saying a currency must have many zeros because one unit is “fundamentally cheap.” The important distinction is between: 1. **The nominal unit** — what one “dong,” “dinar,” yen, or dollar is called and how it is subdivided. 2. **The currency’s real purchasing power and exchange value** — what it buys domestically and relative to foreign goods. The first is mostly a convention. The second is economically meaningful. If Vietnam declared: \[ 1{,}000\ \text{old dong} = 1\ \text{new dong} \] then US$1 might change from about 25,000 old dong to 25 new dong. But wages, bank balances, debts, taxes, and prices would all be divided by 1,000 too. A meal that cost 50,000 old dong would cost 50 new dong; a 10 million old-dong salary would become 10,000 new dong. Nothing about Vietnam’s ability to buy imports, export goods, repay foreign debt, or produce goods has changed. ### Why the exchange rate is not a measure of “real worth” Compare these rough examples: - US$1 ≈ 150 Japanese yen - US$1 ≈ 1,300–1,500 Iraqi dinars - US$1 ≈ 15,000–16,000 Indonesian rupiah - US$1 ≈ 25,000 Vietnamese dong - US$1 ≈ 0.9 euro It would be wrong to conclude that the euro is inherently “stronger” than the yen because fewer euros are needed for a dollar. Japan is obviously not poor because its unit is called the yen rather than, say, a “new yen” worth 100 old yen. If Japan redefined: \[ 100\ \text{yen} = 1\ \text{new yen}, \] then US$1 would become roughly 1.5 new yen. Japan would not become richer or its currency more credible. The labels would simply be smaller. ### What *does* determine a currency’s meaningful value? Over time, a currency’s purchasing power and exchange rate are influenced by things such as: - inflation and expected future inflation; - money supply and interest-rate policy; - government deficits and whether they are financed by creating money; - productivity and trade competitiveness; - foreign-currency reserves and external debt; - political stability and institutional trust; - demand by residents and foreigners to hold that currency. Those factors can make a currency lose value. A history of inflation is often why a country ended up with a small unit in the first place. But once the unit is small, keeping it small is not required by economics—it is just a historical convention unless inflation continues. ### The confusing alternative: a true revaluation A government could try to change the exchange rate *without* proportionally changing domestic prices, wages, and balances. For example, it might announce: > “Instead of 25,000 dong per dollar, the dong is now 2,500 dong per dollar.” That would be a **10-fold revaluation**, not a redenomination. It would make Vietnamese goods much more expensive to foreigners and imports much cheaper for Vietnamese buyers, putting pressure on exporters, domestic producers, reserves, and possibly the exchange-rate regime. It is a real economic policy change and may not be sustainable. Redenomination is different: - **Redenomination:** 25,000 old dong/US$ becomes 25 new dong/US$, while all domestic amounts are divided by 1,000. No real value changes. - **Revaluation:** 25,000 dong/US$ becomes 2,500 dong/US$ while the dong unit itself is otherwise unchanged. Real relative prices change, so the economy is affected. So there is a real principle underneath it, but it is not “many zeros are necessary because the currency is cheap.” The principle is that **only relative prices and purchasing power matter**. Changing the unit of measurement alone cannot change them.
