20:12:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is mostly a **mixed-up, non-technical way of speaking**. “High-valued but cheap” combines two different meanings of “value”: - **High numerical denomination / lots of units per dollar:** e.g., 25,000 Vietnamese dong per US$1. This means each *named unit* is small relative to a dollar. - **Cheap currency in the economic sense:** a currency that has low purchasing power, tends to depreciate, or is undervalued relative to some benchmark. Those are not the same thing. For example: - The Vietnamese dong has a **small unit**: one dong is worth a tiny fraction of a US dollar. - But Vietnam’s currency can still be reasonably stable for domestic transactions. - Calling it “high-valued” because the exchange-rate number is high is backwards: a *high number of dong per dollar* means the individual dong unit has a low dollar price. A clearer vocabulary is: | What you mean | Better wording | |---|---| | Many local units equal US$1 | “The currency has a small nominal unit” or “a high units-per-dollar exchange rate.” | | One unit buys little domestically | “Low domestic purchasing power per unit.” | | Currency has lost value over time | “Depreciated” or “has had inflation.” | | Currency is cheap relative to an estimated fair value | “Undervalued” — a contested technical claim. | | The numbers are unwieldy | “The currency may be a candidate for redenomination.” | So there is no paradox. A currency can have: - a **small nominal unit** because of historical convention; - reasonably stable domestic purchasing power today; - and an exchange rate such as 25,000 units per dollar. That is no stranger economically than using cents, grams, or millimeters rather than dollars, kilograms, or meters. The unit size changes the number written down, not the underlying value of what can be bought.
