20:13:10 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=5177 miss=452
20:13:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Because the two things are often **historically correlated**, even though they are not the same thing. A currency may have many units per US dollar because it went through past inflation, devaluations, or currency crises: - Prices rose over time. - The local currency lost value relative to the dollar. - The country did not redenominate. - Result: exchange rates end up in the thousands or tens of thousands per dollar. So a journalist sees “25,000 dong per dollar” or “15,000 rupiah per dollar” and may use it as a quick visual cue for a currency with a history of depreciation or inflation. But the zeros are an **effect of history and unit choice**, not proof that the currency is currently “cheap” or weak. There are several different meanings news articles may intend: ### 1. “Cheap” as in low price per individual unit This is just arithmetic: - 1 Vietnamese dong is worth a tiny fraction of a US cent. - 1 Japanese yen is also worth less than a cent. That tells you almost nothing by itself. A country could redefine 1,000 dong as 1 new dong tomorrow, and the “cheap-looking” unit would disappear with no change in the economy. ### 2. “Cheap” as in depreciated recently A journalist might mean the currency has fallen against the dollar—for example, from 23,000 to 25,000 dong per dollar. That is economically meaningful, but the meaningful fact is the **percentage change**, roughly an 8–9% depreciation—not that the final number contains five digits. The same decline could be written as: - from $0.0000435 per dong to $0.0000400 per dong, or - from 23 to 25 “new dong” per dollar after a 1,000-to-1 redenomination. Same depreciation; different-looking numbers. ### 3. “Cheap” as in undervalued relative to a benchmark Financial analysts sometimes say a currency is “cheap” when they believe it is **undervalued** compared with: - purchasing-power parity (PPP); - its own historical exchange-rate range; - inflation and interest-rate differentials; - trade competitiveness; - a real effective exchange rate index. For example, they may argue that a currency is cheap because local goods and wages appear inexpensive in dollar terms, or because the currency is below its long-run inflation-adjusted average. That can be a real analytical claim—but it is uncertain and contested. It does **not** follow from the number of zeros. ### Why the wording can mislead News writing often prefers vivid shorthand: > “The currency trades at 25,000 per dollar and is cheap.” Readers can easily infer that “25,000” itself proves cheapness. It does not. A more accurate article would distinguish: > “The dong has a small nominal unit—about 25,000 per US dollar—and analysts consider it undervalued on [specified] measures.” In short: lots of zeros can reflect a country’s inflation/devaluation history and can make a currency *look* cheap, so the ideas are often mentioned together. But the zero count is not an economic valuation measure. The relevant questions are whether the currency is falling, whether domestic purchasing power is stable, and whether it is undervalued relative to a stated benchmark.
