20:13:31 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=5626 miss=741
20:13:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is mostly wrong, but it contains a grain of truth. Iran or Venezuela **can legally and technically redenominate** their currencies. Removing zeros does not, by itself, collapse an economy. It is an accounting conversion: - 1,000,000 old units = 1 new unit - prices, wages, savings, debts, taxes, and exchange rates are converted by the same ratio. That alone leaves real purchasing power unchanged. What people often mean, more accurately, is that a country in a severe inflation and trust crisis may have difficulty carrying out a redenomination **credibly and safely**. For Venezuela, for example, repeated redenominations did happen: - In 2008, it removed 3 zeros. - In 2018, it removed 5 zeros. - In 2021, it removed 6 zeros. Those operations did not cause the underlying collapse. Venezuela’s economic problems—hyperinflation, monetary financing, falling confidence, exchange controls, oil-sector decline, sanctions, distorted prices, and institutional weakness—were already present. But redenominations also did not fix them; inflation eventually generated more zeros. Iran has also proposed and legislated plans to change the currency unit, often framed as replacing the rial with the toman and using a conversion such as 10,000 rials = 1 new toman. The practical rollout has been slow because it requires new notes and coins, legal changes, banking and accounting updates, clear public communication, and a monetary environment stable enough that the reform does not rapidly become obsolete. The grain of truth is this: in a fragile economy, redenomination can be risky because it may: - trigger rumors that savings will be confiscated or devalued; - lead people to buy dollars, gold, or goods before conversion; - expose a gap between the official exchange rate and the market rate; - create opportunities for scams, price-rounding, and corruption; - strain banks, retailers, and government payment systems; - fail publicly if inflation continues. But those are problems of **implementation, credibility, and underlying policy**, not a mathematical constraint caused by zeros. A useful way to state it is: > A country cannot solve inflation by removing zeros. But it can remove zeros once it can operate a sufficiently trustworthy and stable monetary system—or even during instability, if it accepts that the benefit may be temporary. So “they literally cannot do it or the economy collapses” is too strong. The more accurate claim is: **they can do it, but without stabilization it may be disruptive, distrusted, and quickly undone by renewed inflation.**
